#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60a38630-03f4-548c-b340-4fcde68748db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292720} {:answer/id #uuid "679f5fae-07a3-5117-bed0-8e4af0509409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292721} {:answer/id #uuid "e1343e64-0f5d-515c-b7db-f4566253a593" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292722} {:answer/id #uuid "1a7b8f63-23fe-501e-9400-8597d549cd94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292723} {:answer/id #uuid "c141f822-aa3a-58bc-bbe3-8a843ae2fe5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a7b8f63-23fe-501e-9400-8597d549cd94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292723} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c093e5c-eb32-58ef-8dd8-6dc41b84f5e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e86eee2a-2a36-501d-8092-4b903ca0e024" :question/math-academy-id "q-122390" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{1}{2-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{1}{2-x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm (-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x))+C \\\\  & =∓\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "199910d0-a546-5dbf-8303-d453dd3a05b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292730} {:answer/id #uuid "2900ee99-267d-5ede-851f-464024e478be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292731} {:answer/id #uuid "59128ef1-2e85-565b-b109-06722de7a1d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=10x-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292732} {:answer/id #uuid "0364b99a-2a69-50fb-a319-287a64ee583d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5{x}^{5}+\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292733} {:answer/id #uuid "761083cb-de89-58d8-b1dd-f3a75f56f9be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+x+\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2900ee99-267d-5ede-851f-464024e478be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292731} :answer-field/id #uuid "f44ccde7-5000-5d85-98d7-850418ec4789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0027455e-0f8d-5d58-b3c8-8d6a1a10e5aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-5985" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{1+25{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{1+25{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=25{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 5{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 5\\int {x}^{2}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{11}{3}.$"}]}
